Hereditary & Genetic Neuropathic Syndromes (Charcot Marie Tooth ATTR)
Neurological & Neurodegenerative
Pes cavus/hammertoes, Inverted champagne bottle leg atrophy, Symmetrical distal weakness/sensory loss

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: NRD-017
Reason for Visit: Acute exacerbation of neuropathic pain and a severe fall secondary to worsening bilateral foot drop.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted a patient who sustained a fall due to "tripping over their own feet," resulting in acute pain and an inability to safely ambulate. Immediate assessment is required to rule out acute fracture following the fall and implement safe mobility strategies before the next available in-hours appointment.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My feet keep catching on the carpet. I fell hard on my knee today and the burning in my toes is worse than ever."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports an insidious, slowly progressive onset of distal limb muscle weakness and noticeable gross atrophy over many years, consistent with a known hereditary neuropathy. Today, the patient suffered a mechanical fall directly attributed to a symmetrical, bilateral foot drop resulting in a prominent, slapping steppage gait. The fall caused acute pain in the right knee. Concurrently, the patient reports an exacerbation of distal sensory loss affecting proprioception, vibration, and distinct pinprick sensation in a stocking-and-glove distribution, leading to a deep burning neuropathic pain that is disrupting sleep. There is a strong, undeniable family history of similar progressively severe neuropathic traits. The patient exhibits severe, fixed foot deformities including pronounced pes cavus (high arches) and severe hammertoes, which make standard footwear impossible to use safely.
Functional Status: Mobilizing with high risk of falls; severely restricted by distal weakness and deformities.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone; high risk of recurrent falls and subsequent fractures.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 135/85 mmHg | HR: 78 bpm | Temp: 36.6 C | RR: 14 bpm | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 68 Kg
General: Alert, comfortable at rest, but visibly frustrated by physical limitations.
Physical Exam: Lower extremities reveal classic inverted champagne bottle appearance of the lower legs due to severe distal muscle wasting. Bilateral pes cavus and hammertoes are prominent. Neurological exam confirms symmetrical distal weakness (ankle dorsiflexion 2/5 bilaterally) and universally diminished or completely absent deep tendon reflexes, particularly at the ankles. Sensation to vibration and pinprick is severely reduced to the mid-calf bilaterally. Examination of the right knee reveals a superficial abrasion and mild soft tissue swelling, but active range of motion is intact, joint is stable, and there is no bony tenderness.
Environmental Observations: Loose rugs throughout the hallway; no grab bars in the bathroom.
Medication Reconciliation: Paracetamol 1000mg taken post-fall with minimal relief of the burning neuropathic pain.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Hereditary Motor and Sensory Neuropathy (Charcot-Marie-Tooth) - Status: Chronic, with acute fall and neuropathic pain flare
Differentials:

Acute Tibial Plateau Fracture - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Patient can fully bear weight on the right leg post-fall, active range of motion is preserved, and there is no exquisite focal bony tenderness.

Diabetic Peripheral Neuropathy - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Presence of severe, chronic structural foot deformities (pes cavus), dramatic distal muscle atrophy (inverted champagne bottle), and clear autosomal dominant family history.

Lumbar Radiculopathy (L5) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Bilateral, highly symmetrical presentation of weakness, severe long-standing atrophy, and stocking-glove sensory loss rather than a specific dermatomal distribution.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient experienced a mechanical fall directly attributable to the progressive bilateral foot drop characteristic of their hereditary neuropathy. The acute knee pain and the inability to safely mobilize independently necessitated an urgent after-hours evaluation. Delaying assessment would have subjected the patient to severe anxiety regarding a potential fracture and left them at an unacceptably high risk for a second, more catastrophic fall overnight. Urgent assessment successfully ruled out an acute fracture, allowed for the immediate prescription of neuropathic analgesia for the burning pain, and facilitated critical, immediate environmental modifications and mobility advice to ensure safety until allied health interventions could be arranged.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed Pregabalin 75mg at night for neuropathic pain control. Recommended simple analgesics (Ibuprofen 400mg TDS) for the acute soft tissue knee injury.
Positive Behavioral Change:
Step 1: By strictly wearing rigid, high-top, lace-up boots indoors instead of slippers, the patient artificially braces the ankle joint, immediately neutralizing the foot drop and preventing further tripping.
Step 2: By immediately removing all loose throw rugs and electrical cords from walking pathways, the patient actively modifies the environment, directly eliminating the primary obstacles that catch the dropped foot.
Step 3: By consciously lifting the knees higher with every step (exaggerated marching gait), the patient forces the toes to clear the floor, reliably preventing the scuffing and catching that causes falls.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Urgent referral to community physiotherapy and orthotics for custom Ankle-Foot Orthoses (AFOs).
DME/Safety (Optional): Four-wheeled walker (rollator) recommended for outdoor use.
Follow-up: Routine GP follow-up in 1 week to review neuropathic pain control and allied health referrals.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Attend the Emergency Department if you become entirely unable to bear weight on the right leg, if the knee swells massively, or if you experience a sudden loss of bowel or bladder control.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Hereditary neuropathies are slowly progressive; the current focus is entirely on symptom management and fall prevention.
Clinical Course: The acute knee pain will resolve over 1-2 weeks. The underlying foot drop is permanent and will require long-term bracing (AFOs) to restore safe, independent walking.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Return for medical review if the nerve pain in your feet completely fails to respond to the new medication, or if you develop any open sores or ulcers on the deformed areas of your toes.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The genetic condition causes the long nerves to your feet to slowly die back. This wastes the muscles that lift your foot, causing it to drag and trip you. Regular painkillers don't fix nerve pain, so we are starting a specific medication that calms the misfiring nerves so you can sleep.
Actionable Steps: Clear the floors of all rugs right now. Wear supportive boots indoors. Start the Pregabalin tonight.

